WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:03.977
This briefing comes from
DNS, DHCP and IPAM,

3
00:00:03.977 --> 00:00:07.975
one of the books of the
Federal Modernization Workbook.

4
00:00:07.975 --> 00:00:12.411
It's about governing the name and
address layer as one control plane,

5
00:00:12.411 --> 00:00:15.975
across the data center, every
cloud and the branch edge.

6
00:00:15.975 --> 00:00:20.212
That may sound like plumbing, but here's
the idea that drives the whole book.

7
00:00:20.212 --> 00:00:22.350
Every connection starts with a name.

8
00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:25.650
So whoever governs the name
layer governs the enterprise.

9
00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:29.471
We'll go in four steps: why
DNS is now the control plane,

10
00:00:29.471 --> 00:00:33.825
what has to change, who owns it,
and how to migrate it and run it.

11
00:00:33.825 --> 00:00:38.635
Along the way you'll hear about failures
that don't look like DNS failures at all,

12
00:00:38.635 --> 00:00:41.225
which is exactly why
they're so hard to find.

13
00:00:41.225 --> 00:00:42.830
Let's start with why.

14
00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:50.880
Almost every network connection
begins with a DNS query.

15
00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:52.743
That's been true for a long time.

16
00:00:52.743 --> 00:00:55.480
What's changed is how much
the answer now decides.

17
00:00:55.480 --> 00:01:01.642
In a hybrid, multi-cloud estate, DNS does
six jobs its designers never planned for.

18
00:01:01.642 --> 00:01:04.799
For example, one storage
name can answer privately

19
00:01:04.799 --> 00:01:07.880
inside its network and
publicly everywhere else.

20
00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:11.373
DNS also picks which SaaS
front door a user reaches,

21
00:01:11.373 --> 00:01:15.032
blocks known-bad domains
before a connection is made,

22
00:01:15.032 --> 00:01:20.355
and carries certificate checks, because
revocation endpoints are DNS names.

23
00:01:20.355 --> 00:01:22.218
The slide lists all six.

24
00:01:22.218 --> 00:01:24.882
NIST's 2026 guide puts it plainly:

25
00:01:24.882 --> 00:01:29.518
DNS has become more than a
service; it's a security control.

26
00:01:29.518 --> 00:01:34.367
Yet many estates run it on as many as
five platforms that share no state.

27
00:01:34.367 --> 00:01:39.955
The answer is one authoritative DDI policy
plane, a requirement, not a product.

28
00:01:39.955 --> 00:01:42.320
Here's what goes wrong without one.

29
00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:49.559
Run those platforms separately
and you get split-brain DNS:

30
00:01:49.559 --> 00:01:54.045
the same name giving different answers
depending on where the query starts.

31
00:01:54.045 --> 00:01:55.658
A designed split is fine.

32
00:01:55.658 --> 00:02:00.107
The problem is unplanned drift
between systems with no shared state.

33
00:02:00.107 --> 00:02:01.695
Here's the classic case.

34
00:02:01.695 --> 00:02:04.434
A workload in AWS only
gets an Azure private

35
00:02:04.434 --> 00:02:07.857
endpoint's private address
through a forwarding rule.

36
00:02:07.857 --> 00:02:10.022
If that rule is missing or stale,

37
00:02:10.022 --> 00:02:13.519
the name still resolves,
just to the public address.

38
00:02:13.519 --> 00:02:16.694
Microsoft attributes
roughly seventy percent of

39
00:02:16.694 --> 00:02:20.095
private-endpoint access-denied
cases to exactly that.

40
00:02:20.095 --> 00:02:22.357
The same drift happens with SaaS paths,

41
00:02:22.357 --> 00:02:26.457
and with security blocks that only
apply on the resolver that answers.

42
00:02:26.457 --> 00:02:30.770
Either way, the failure is silent,
and it shows up as something else.

43
00:02:30.770 --> 00:02:34.382
Changes come faster than any
manual process can track.

44
00:02:34.382 --> 00:02:38.407
Only a control plane that holds the
policy keeps everything in step.

45
00:02:38.407 --> 00:02:41.040
So what do the mandates actually require?

46
00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:47.308
The federal mandates never name a product,

47
00:02:47.308 --> 00:02:50.690
but read together they describe
one governed name layer.

48
00:02:50.690 --> 00:02:53.440
It's worth being precise
about who requires what.

49
00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:57.260
OMB requires encrypted DNS
wherever it's supported,

50
00:02:57.260 --> 00:03:00.903
with endpoints pointed
at designated resolvers.

51
00:03:00.903 --> 00:03:04.479
The requirement to send
outbound resolution through

52
00:03:04.479 --> 00:03:07.578
Protective DNS comes from
CISA's own guidance.

53
00:03:07.578 --> 00:03:11.594
The current logging memo,
M-26-14, doesn't name DNS,

54
00:03:11.594 --> 00:03:17.052
but its logging and retention
requirements land squarely on it.

55
00:03:17.052 --> 00:03:19.086
Other sources carry DNSSEC,

56
00:03:19.086 --> 00:03:24.302
and the latest TIC 3.0 catalog
lists five DNS capabilities.

57
00:03:24.302 --> 00:03:26.102
The slide has the full list.

58
00:03:26.102 --> 00:03:31.703
NIST's March 2026 revision of its DNS
deployment guide ties it together,

59
00:03:31.703 --> 00:03:34.077
but it's guidance, not a mandate.

60
00:03:34.077 --> 00:03:38.031
And FedRAMP certifies a cloud
service, not an architecture,

61
00:03:38.031 --> 00:03:41.340
so the agency still authorizes
its own DDI system.

62
00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:45.520
Now, the biggest thing that has
to change: Active Directory.

63
00:03:49.270 --> 00:03:52.379
For twenty years, Active
Directory has been the

64
00:03:52.379 --> 00:03:55.408
hidden DNS service of
the on-premises network.

65
00:03:55.408 --> 00:03:58.385
A domain's name becomes a
zone, and every domain-joined

66
00:03:58.385 --> 00:04:01.633
machine points at the domain
controllers for everything.

67
00:04:01.633 --> 00:04:06.094
Each domain controller registers the
locator records clients use to find it,

68
00:04:06.094 --> 00:04:08.744
and secure dynamic
updates keep them current.

69
00:04:08.744 --> 00:04:11.370
Entra ID inherits none of that.

70
00:04:11.370 --> 00:04:14.945
It hosts no zones and
publishes no locator records.

71
00:04:14.945 --> 00:04:18.945
Yet hybrid-joined devices still go
looking for a domain controller.

72
00:04:18.945 --> 00:04:20.445
So the plan is careful.

73
00:04:20.445 --> 00:04:25.832
The locator records stay where domain
controllers can register them securely,

74
00:04:25.832 --> 00:04:29.995
either on AD DNS or a DDI platform
that accepts secure updates,

75
00:04:29.995 --> 00:04:32.607
until the last domain controller goes.

76
00:04:32.607 --> 00:04:36.558
Everything else moves zone by
zone, once it's been inventoried.

77
00:04:36.558 --> 00:04:41.290
But even a well-designed name layer only
works if queries actually reach it.

78
00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:49.077
Every control in the policy plane
assumes the query reaches it.

79
00:04:49.077 --> 00:04:51.680
Three ordinary things
decide whether it does,

80
00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:53.940
and none of them tells
you when it doesn't.

81
00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:55.815
First, the branch edge.

82
00:04:55.815 --> 00:05:01.146
SD-WAN can redirect branch DNS to a
designated resolver, but that target has

83
00:05:01.146 --> 00:05:06.477
to be a real agency resolver on the
network, not an agent on the device itself.

84
00:05:06.477 --> 00:05:09.003
Second, encrypted DNS.

85
00:05:09.003 --> 00:05:13.177
Some forms can be blocked at the
firewall except to approved resolvers.

86
00:05:13.177 --> 00:05:16.706
DNS over HTTPS hides
inside normal web traffic,

87
00:05:16.706 --> 00:05:22.315
so it has to be governed on the endpoint,
through browser and Windows policy.

88
00:05:22.315 --> 00:05:24.640
Third, rogue DHCP.

89
00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:29.177
One rogue server handing out its own
resolver can move a whole subnet at once.

90
00:05:29.177 --> 00:05:33.278
AD authorization doesn't stop
that; switch protections do.

91
00:05:33.278 --> 00:05:37.610
Next, a dependency that
surprises people: certificates.

92
00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:46.573
A certificate can be valid, unrevoked and
issued for the right name, and still fail.

93
00:05:46.573 --> 00:05:51.411
That's because every check that makes it
trustworthy starts with a DNS lookup.

94
00:05:51.411 --> 00:05:52.747
Here's a real pattern.

95
00:05:52.747 --> 00:05:57.860
An agency's revocation list
name exists only in AD DNS.

96
00:05:57.860 --> 00:06:01.910
A workload in AWS can't resolve
it without a forwarding rule.

97
00:06:01.910 --> 00:06:06.545
The revocation check fails, a ticket
gets opened against the PKI team,

98
00:06:06.545 --> 00:06:09.147
and the real cause can take days to find.

99
00:06:09.147 --> 00:06:10.984
DNSSEC is the other half.

100
00:06:10.984 --> 00:06:14.447
It proves where an answer came
from and that it wasn't changed,

101
00:06:14.447 --> 00:06:16.248
but it doesn't encrypt anything.

102
00:06:16.248 --> 00:06:18.135
The program follows NIST.

103
00:06:18.135 --> 00:06:21.835
Always sign external zones
and the dot-gov delegations.

104
00:06:21.835 --> 00:06:24.422
Validate on every agency resolver.

105
00:06:24.422 --> 00:06:28.610
Sign internal zones only where the
system's authorization requires it.

106
00:06:28.610 --> 00:06:31.149
Validation stays on agency resolvers,

107
00:06:31.149 --> 00:06:34.323
because the cloud resolvers
don't fully cover it.

108
00:06:34.323 --> 00:06:37.550
Next, the most common
source of split-brain.

109
00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:46.013
Azure private endpoints are the most common
source of private-address split-brain.

110
00:06:46.013 --> 00:06:50.270
When you create one, Azure writes
its record into a private zone,

111
00:06:50.270 --> 00:06:52.726
and public DNS points into that name.

112
00:06:52.726 --> 00:06:54.876
You only get the private answer if your

113
00:06:54.876 --> 00:06:57.363
query reaches a resolver
linked to that zone.

114
00:06:57.363 --> 00:06:58.875
So the rule is simple.

115
00:06:58.875 --> 00:07:03.208
Every origin, whether on-premises,
another cloud or the branch,

116
00:07:03.208 --> 00:07:08.050
forwards the service's public zone
to the Azure DNS Private Resolver.

117
00:07:08.050 --> 00:07:09.325
Not the private zone.

118
00:07:09.325 --> 00:07:12.090
And Azure Government uses
different zone names,

119
00:07:12.090 --> 00:07:14.163
so commercial rules don't carry over.

120
00:07:14.163 --> 00:07:17.492
The Private Resolver is a
bridge, not an authority,

121
00:07:17.492 --> 00:07:22.526
so you reach it by forwarding, never by
delegation, and deploy one per region.

122
00:07:22.526 --> 00:07:24.775
Addresses need the same discipline,

123
00:07:24.775 --> 00:07:29.875
with the enterprise IPAM as the authority
and the clouds reconciled against it.

124
00:07:29.875 --> 00:07:33.200
Put it together, and you
get one governed path.

125
00:07:36.950 --> 00:07:39.166
Here's the whole path in one picture.

126
00:07:39.166 --> 00:07:43.763
A branch client's query goes from the
SD-WAN edge to an agency resolver,

127
00:07:43.763 --> 00:07:48.280
then through the DDI policy plane,
then to each cloud's private zones.

128
00:07:48.280 --> 00:07:52.372
Internet-bound lookups leave
through CISA's Protective DNS.

129
00:07:52.372 --> 00:07:55.953
Five decisions carry almost
all the operational risk.

130
00:07:55.953 --> 00:07:58.722
The edge points at a real agency resolver.

131
00:07:58.722 --> 00:08:01.347
That resolver is always the first hop.

132
00:08:01.347 --> 00:08:04.624
The policy plane holds
every cloud forwarding rule.

133
00:08:04.624 --> 00:08:10.177
AD DNS is inventoried and rebuilt
before any domain controller goes.

134
00:08:10.177 --> 00:08:13.123
And revocation endpoints
resolve from everywhere.

135
00:08:13.123 --> 00:08:16.771
What makes these dangerous is
that each one fails silently.

136
00:08:16.771 --> 00:08:21.419
The query still resolves, just outside
policy or to the wrong address.

137
00:08:21.419 --> 00:08:24.584
So each decision comes with
a check, run against the live

138
00:08:24.584 --> 00:08:28.734
configuration rather than the design
document, before the next change.

139
00:08:28.734 --> 00:08:31.250
Which brings us to who owns all this.

140
00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:39.926
In an AD estate, DNS belonged to
whoever ran the domain controllers,

141
00:08:39.926 --> 00:08:42.075
and nobody really chose that.

142
00:08:42.075 --> 00:08:45.172
A single policy plane
concentrates that authority,

143
00:08:45.172 --> 00:08:49.350
so its owners have to be named on
purpose, with one decider per decision.

144
00:08:49.350 --> 00:08:52.656
The sponsor and CIO decide the platform

145
00:08:52.656 --> 00:08:56.363
model and when AD DNS
and Windows DHCP retire.

146
00:08:56.363 --> 00:08:59.377
The CISO and ISSO set the requirements,

147
00:08:59.377 --> 00:09:02.938
like designated resolvers,
DNSSEC and logging.

148
00:09:02.938 --> 00:09:08.425
The authorizing official accepts residual
risk, including every standing exception.

149
00:09:08.425 --> 00:09:13.525
The DNS and DDI team runs the plane
and decides what the edge points at.

150
00:09:13.525 --> 00:09:17.325
Network engineering configures
it, and cloud teams consume it.

151
00:09:17.325 --> 00:09:19.687
Record changes come in three classes.

152
00:09:19.687 --> 00:09:23.350
Routine changes in a delegated
namespace are pre-approved.

153
00:09:23.350 --> 00:09:26.575
Policy-driven records follow
a change-controlled policy.

154
00:09:26.575 --> 00:09:28.837
And high-impact changes, like zones,

155
00:09:28.837 --> 00:09:32.112
delegations and forwarding,
go to the change board.

156
00:09:32.112 --> 00:09:33.740
So how do we get there?

157
00:09:37.490 --> 00:09:42.382
The migration runs in six phases,
from inventory and coexistence

158
00:09:42.382 --> 00:09:47.816
through to decommissioning AD DNS and
DHCP, then hardening and evidence.

159
00:09:47.816 --> 00:09:50.515
Each phase ends with
a rollback point and a

160
00:09:50.515 --> 00:09:53.992
resolution test from every
origin, not a calendar date.

161
00:09:53.992 --> 00:09:57.334
Every move, whether it's
a zone, a forwarder,

162
00:09:57.334 --> 00:10:01.357
a DHCP scope or a wave of
clients, runs the same loop.

163
00:10:01.357 --> 00:10:03.394
Lower the time-to-live well ahead.

164
00:10:03.394 --> 00:10:08.134
Import with a dry run, never moving
the core zone or locator records.

165
00:10:08.134 --> 00:10:09.757
Validate from everywhere.

166
00:10:09.757 --> 00:10:13.673
Then switch, and watch, while the
old authority keeps answering.

167
00:10:13.673 --> 00:10:17.189
One warning from Azure: removing
a network link deletes the

168
00:10:17.189 --> 00:10:20.560
records registered through it,
and they can't be restored.

169
00:10:20.560 --> 00:10:23.375
And decommissioning is
gated, never scheduled.

170
00:10:23.375 --> 00:10:27.711
The old servers retire only after
the records resolve cleanly for a

171
00:10:27.711 --> 00:10:32.125
sustained period, parallel operation
passes, and failover is tested.

172
00:10:32.125 --> 00:10:34.720
Then the plane has to run every day.

173
00:10:38.470 --> 00:10:41.433
One policy plane is only an
improvement if divisions

174
00:10:41.433 --> 00:10:43.858
can still run their own
networks through it.

175
00:10:43.858 --> 00:10:47.380
So each division gets a
scope over its own zones,

176
00:10:47.380 --> 00:10:51.895
addresses and DHCP, with manager,
operator and auditor roles.

177
00:10:51.895 --> 00:10:54.345
Automation is held to the same scopes.

178
00:10:54.345 --> 00:10:58.563
Infrastructure as code, ticketing
workflows and certificate

179
00:10:58.563 --> 00:11:02.132
automation all go through
one API and one audit trail.

180
00:11:02.132 --> 00:11:04.942
Locator records are
checked from every site,

181
00:11:04.942 --> 00:11:09.044
every fifteen minutes, until the
last domain controller retires.

182
00:11:09.044 --> 00:11:12.765
Drift reports compare the source
of truth with every copy,

183
00:11:12.765 --> 00:11:16.020
and hygiene jobs catch
stale and dangling records.

184
00:11:16.020 --> 00:11:18.808
The evidence floor is
the federal logging memo.

185
00:11:18.808 --> 00:11:22.733
It doesn't name DNS, but its
retention rules apply to it.

186
00:11:22.733 --> 00:11:25.527
Query logs, including encrypted queries,

187
00:11:25.527 --> 00:11:29.144
and DHCP lease history are
how the name layer meets it.

188
00:11:29.144 --> 00:11:32.783
And lease history is what
turns an address into a device.

189
00:11:32.783 --> 00:11:35.020
Now, choosing the platform.

190
00:11:38.770 --> 00:11:41.645
Choose the platform on
the evidence it produces

191
00:11:41.645 --> 00:11:43.945
for your own boundary, not on a brochure.

192
00:11:43.945 --> 00:11:47.015
Commercial DDI pricing
is quoted per agency,

193
00:11:47.015 --> 00:11:49.920
so get every quote on
the same assumptions.

194
00:11:49.920 --> 00:11:54.482
The cloud-native pieces have public
prices and show up in every model.

195
00:11:54.482 --> 00:11:59.858
As one example, a hub with one inbound
and one outbound Azure resolver endpoint

196
00:11:59.858 --> 00:12:04.820
costs about 362 dollars a month per
region, and a second region doubles it.

197
00:12:04.820 --> 00:12:09.307
Remember that authorization belongs
to a specific offering, not a vendor.

198
00:12:09.307 --> 00:12:12.389
The slide shows which DDI
offerings have a FedRAMP

199
00:12:12.389 --> 00:12:16.132
authorization and which run
inside the agency's own boundary.

200
00:12:16.132 --> 00:12:20.483
And a Moderate authorization
doesn't cover GCC High or DoD.

201
00:12:20.483 --> 00:12:22.346
Some risk remains either way.

202
00:12:22.346 --> 00:12:25.457
Sync lag becomes the new
source of divergence.

203
00:12:25.457 --> 00:12:29.482
A management outage blocks changes,
though resolution keeps working.

204
00:12:29.482 --> 00:12:34.996
And encrypted DNS bypass stays possible
where endpoint controls are incomplete.

205
00:12:34.996 --> 00:12:36.980
So, the decisions.

206
00:12:40.730 --> 00:12:42.330
Here are the four decisions.

207
00:12:42.330 --> 00:12:47.176
First, authorize a single authoritative
DDI policy plane and give it an owner,

208
00:12:47.176 --> 00:12:51.492
typically the enterprise architecture
or network operations lead.

209
00:12:51.492 --> 00:12:56.371
Second, have that owner settle the five
configuration decisions within ninety days,

210
00:12:56.371 --> 00:12:59.717
and report the residual risk
to the authorizing official.

211
00:12:59.717 --> 00:13:02.299
Third, choose the platform on evidence:

212
00:13:02.299 --> 00:13:07.380
authorization at the right impact level,
and quotes on the same assumptions.

213
00:13:07.380 --> 00:13:11.068
And fourth, name the owners,
one decider per decision.

214
00:13:11.068 --> 00:13:13.980
The next step is Phase 1, the inventory.

215
00:13:13.980 --> 00:13:19.946
Export every AD DNS zone, locator record,
forwarder and DHCP scope, every edge

216
00:13:19.946 --> 00:13:23.733
redirect target and every
revocation endpoint,

217
00:13:23.733 --> 00:13:27.142
before anyone touches a domain controller.

218
00:13:27.142 --> 00:13:31.617
It's read-only, and it's the one step
whose omission can't be repaired later.

219
00:13:31.617 --> 00:13:33.950
Govern the name layer as one plane,

220
00:13:33.950 --> 00:13:38.030
and it can carry the cloud,
branch and identity transitions.

221
00:13:38.030 --> 00:13:43.668
DNS, DHCP and IPAM has the
detail, every claim footnoted.

222
00:13:43.668 --> 00:13:44.500
Thank you.
